# Migrations are hashed, so their bytes are part of their identity.
#
# scripts/migrate.mjs records sha256 of each migration's content and refuses to
# run against a database where a recorded file no longer hashes the same --
# correctly, because an edited migration means the schema and the repository
# disagree and no later migration reconciles that.
#
# That makes line endings load-bearing. This repository has core.autocrlf=true
# and had no .gitattributes, so `git checkout` on Windows rewrote LF to CRLF and
# changed the hash. It happened to exactly one file during the 0055 work, and
# `migrate.mjs` would then have refused to apply anything against production,
# reporting "0050_survey_participation.sql has changed since it was applied" for
# a file nobody had edited.
#
# eol=lf pins the checkout to LF, which is what the committed blobs already are
# and what production's ledger was computed from -- verified at the time this
# was added: production's recorded checksum matched the committed blob for all
# 54 applied migrations, and the working tree for 53 of them, the difference
# being that one converted file.
#
# So this changes no committed byte and no recorded checksum. It makes a
# Windows checkout produce the same bytes a Linux one does, which is the only
# way the hash can mean anything.
*.sql text eol=lf
